    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to calculate the maximum recommended bag mass before comparing it with the estimated mean bag mass. Ask one focused question without giving the calculation.",
      "content": "What calculation would you use to find 10% of the mean body mass, 80 kg?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think 10% of 80 is 10 kg, so maybe the bags are within the guideline?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner recognises that a percentage of 80 is needed but has treated 10% as approximately 10 kg. Clarify that 10% means one tenth, calculate the limit, then compare it with the estimated mean bag mass. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.10 * 80\") -> 8.0]",
      "content": "Here, 10% means one tenth, so the guideline limit is **8 kg**, not 10 kg. Since the estimated mean bag mass is **10.68 kg**, which is greater than 8 kg, the bags are **not satisfying** the guideline on average.\n\n**Final answer:** No, $10.68\\text{ kg} > 8\\text{ kg}$.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $L = \\frac{p}{100} \\times M$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $L$: the maximum recommended bag mass\n  * $p$: the guideline percentage\n  * $M$: the mean body mass\n* **Why we use it:** The guideline sets the bag limit as a percentage of body mass. We find 10% of 80 kg, giving $L = \\frac{10}{100} \\times 80 = 8\\text{ kg}$, then compare the mean bag mass with this limit.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Show both the calculated limit and the comparison with the mean bag mass to motivate your yes-or-no answer."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "socratic_hints",
    "final_answer": "No, 10.68 kg > 8 kg",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nlimit = sp.Rational(10, 100) * 80\nbag_mean = sp.Rational(1068, 100)\nprint(f\"No, {bag_mean} kg > {limit} kg\")",
    "techniques": [
      "calculate_percentage",
      "compare_mean_with_guideline"
    ]
  }
